Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 04/13/2023] 
1.  Er = 21 keV: partial widths derived from La Cognata et al. 2008, Champagne & Pitt 1986, and priv. comm. by Allen, quoted in Wiescher et al. 1980.
2.  Er = 91 keV: partial widths derived from Best 2019; Bayesian average of Lorenz-Wirzba 1979, La Cognata 2008, Bruno 2019; and priv. comm. by Allen, quoted in 
    Wiescher et al. 1980. 
3.  Er = 145 keV: partial widths derived from (p,g) strength [many measurements], and (p,a) strength and total width reported in Becker 1995.
4.  Up to Er=2 MeV, input data are taken from the same source as for the 18O(p,g)19F reaction (see previous table), except for comments below; for higher energies 
    the partial widths are adopted from Sellin et al. 1969, Orihara et al. 1973, Almanza et al. 1975 and Murillo et al. 1979.
5.  For energies and partial widths of broad "657 keV" and 800" keV resonances, we adopted the average from Yagi 1962, Mak et al. 1978, and La Cognata et al. 2010, 
    and Bruno 2019. Note that: (i) the weighted resonance energy from the R-matrix fits [628 keV] does not agree with the values obtained from the excitation energy 
    in ENSDF [657 keV]; (ii) we assume un uncertainty of 25% for the weighted mean of the partial widths. Some of the reported uncertainties for Gp and Ga see too 
    optimistic [with uncertainties in the sub-% range].
6.  Interferences between 145 and 628 keV resonances: we assumed that the two-level interference is destructive (constructive) outside (inside) the 145-628 keV energy 
    region (see Becker et al. 1995 and Lorenz-Wirzba et al. 1979). 

